1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,196
What happens when the price of a

2
00:00:02,196 --> 00:00:04,712
metal doubles in a single morning,

3
00:00:04,712 --> 00:00:07,251
and the market where it trades decides

4
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:09,466
that none of it happened?

5
00:00:14,717 --> 00:00:16,626
In March twenty twenty-two,

6
00:00:16,626 --> 00:00:18,917
it happened in London.

7
00:00:18,957 --> 00:00:20,451
The price of nickel,

8
00:00:20,451 --> 00:00:22,984
a metal used in stainless steel and

9
00:00:22,984 --> 00:00:23,972
batteries,

10
00:00:23,972 --> 00:00:26,251
shot past one hundred and one thousand

11
00:00:26,251 --> 00:00:28,784
three hundred and sixty-five dollars a

12
00:00:28,784 --> 00:00:29,637
tonne.

13
00:00:30,007 --> 00:00:32,065
The London Metal Exchange suspended

14
00:00:32,065 --> 00:00:33,077
trading.

15
00:00:33,117 --> 00:00:35,631
Then it cancelled every trade made that

16
00:00:35,631 --> 00:00:36,293
morning,

17
00:00:36,293 --> 00:00:38,350
worth about thirteen point four billion

18
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:39,407
dollars.

19
00:00:39,657 --> 00:00:41,787
Some traders lost fortunes they thought

20
00:00:41,787 --> 00:00:43,097
they had made.

21
00:00:43,187 --> 00:00:45,635
Others were saved from ruin.

22
00:00:51,283 --> 00:00:53,312
Who sets the price of the world's

23
00:00:53,312 --> 00:00:54,492
metals?

24
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:02,500
For most of the metals in your

25
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:03,027
phone,

26
00:01:03,027 --> 00:01:05,216
your car or your kitchen sink,

27
00:01:05,216 --> 00:01:08,033
a key price is set in London.

28
00:01:08,373 --> 00:01:10,894
The London Metal Exchange trades contracts

29
00:01:10,894 --> 00:01:12,475
to deliver copper,

30
00:01:12,475 --> 00:01:13,598
aluminium,

31
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:15,409
nickel and other metals,

32
00:01:15,409 --> 00:01:17,613
usually three months ahead.

33
00:01:17,823 --> 00:01:18,646
Miners,

34
00:01:18,646 --> 00:01:21,485
factories and traders use those contracts

35
00:01:21,485 --> 00:01:24,039
to protect themselves against price

36
00:01:24,039 --> 00:01:24,923
swings.

37
00:01:25,133 --> 00:01:27,546
And the market runs almost around the

38
00:01:27,546 --> 00:01:28,004
clock,

39
00:01:28,004 --> 00:01:30,659
with seven of its nineteen trading hours

40
00:01:30,659 --> 00:01:33,189
handled overnight from Hong Kong.

41
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:35,889
Let me draw how a short position

42
00:01:35,889 --> 00:01:36,760
works.

43
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:39,189
A company that will sell nickel in

44
00:01:39,189 --> 00:01:41,900
the future agrees today to deliver it

45
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:43,630
at a fixed price.

46
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:45,320
That is a short.

47
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:47,082
If the price falls,

48
00:01:47,082 --> 00:01:48,320
it wins.

49
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:49,883
If the price rises,

50
00:01:49,883 --> 00:01:50,882
it loses,

51
00:01:50,882 --> 00:01:52,933
and the exchange asks it to put

52
00:01:52,933 --> 00:01:55,497
up more cash every day to cover

53
00:01:55,497 --> 00:01:56,610
the loss.

54
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:59,190
That cash is called margin.

55
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:01,834
And if the price rises fast enough,

56
00:02:01,834 --> 00:02:04,101
the margin calls can grow faster than

57
00:02:04,101 --> 00:02:05,709
anyone can pay.

58
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:08,253
In early twenty twenty-two,

59
00:02:08,253 --> 00:02:10,323
there was a very large short in

60
00:02:10,323 --> 00:02:11,440
nickel.

61
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:13,856
According to the claimants in the later

62
00:02:13,856 --> 00:02:14,485
court case,

63
00:02:14,485 --> 00:02:16,655
it was built up by companies within

64
00:02:16,655 --> 00:02:17,284
Tsingshan,

65
00:02:17,284 --> 00:02:20,270
a Chinese industrial user of nickel.

66
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:21,928
And much of it was held not

67
00:02:21,928 --> 00:02:23,474
on the exchange itself,

68
00:02:23,474 --> 00:02:25,490
but in private deals between banks and

69
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:26,140
traders,

70
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:28,980
called the over the counter market.

71
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,775
The exchange could not see it.

72
00:02:32,017 --> 00:02:32,504
Then,

73
00:02:32,504 --> 00:02:34,531
at the end of February,

74
00:02:34,531 --> 00:02:36,927
Russia invaded Ukraine.

75
00:02:36,987 --> 00:02:39,085
And as one of the exchange's own

76
00:02:39,085 --> 00:02:40,461
board members would note,

77
00:02:40,461 --> 00:02:43,025
Russia is a large global producer of

78
00:02:43,025 --> 00:02:44,092
nickel.

79
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:52,654
What happens when everyone who bet on

80
00:02:52,654 --> 00:02:55,287
a fall has to buy at once?

81
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:02,080
The pressure built over a few days.

82
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:02,989
On Friday,

83
00:03:02,989 --> 00:03:03,665
March fourth,

84
00:03:03,665 --> 00:03:05,995
the exchange's clearing house asked its

85
00:03:05,995 --> 00:03:08,093
members for a record three point five

86
00:03:08,093 --> 00:03:10,460
billion dollars in extra margin.

87
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:11,954
On Monday morning,

88
00:03:11,954 --> 00:03:14,486
it asked for another five point one

89
00:03:14,486 --> 00:03:15,727
billion dollars,

90
00:03:15,727 --> 00:03:17,150
a new record.

91
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:18,971
By the end of Monday,

92
00:03:18,971 --> 00:03:22,137
nickel had jumped by sixty-six percent in

93
00:03:22,137 --> 00:03:23,430
a single day,

94
00:03:23,430 --> 00:03:26,069
to forty-eight thousand and seventy-five

95
00:03:26,069 --> 00:03:27,725
dollars a tonne.

96
00:03:27,983 --> 00:03:29,401
Inside the exchange,

97
00:03:29,401 --> 00:03:31,353
people were alarmed.

98
00:03:31,353 --> 00:03:33,827
By two in the afternoon on Monday,

99
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:35,870
the clearing house had called more than

100
00:03:35,870 --> 00:03:38,141
seven point four billion dollars from its

101
00:03:38,141 --> 00:03:39,253
members that day,

102
00:03:39,253 --> 00:03:41,750
and one manager warned in an email

103
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:43,566
that the calls were adding to the

104
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:44,393
stress.

105
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:46,416
A board member wrote that they did

106
00:03:46,416 --> 00:03:49,091
not understand why nickel was up sixty-six

107
00:03:49,091 --> 00:03:51,743
percent while aluminium was down three,

108
00:03:51,743 --> 00:03:53,903
when Russia produced both.

109
00:03:53,983 --> 00:03:56,224
Another described the metals markets to

110
00:03:56,224 --> 00:03:59,252
the British Treasury as in total disarray.

111
00:03:59,517 --> 00:04:01,567
Then came the night.

112
00:04:01,597 --> 00:04:04,433
Trading reopened in the early hours of

113
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:05,180
Tuesday,

114
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:06,186
London time,

115
00:04:06,186 --> 00:04:07,817
run from Hong Kong.

116
00:04:07,867 --> 00:04:10,652
The exchange had automatic limits,

117
00:04:10,652 --> 00:04:11,903
called price bands,

118
00:04:11,903 --> 00:04:14,547
to stop prices moving too fast.

119
00:04:14,927 --> 00:04:17,162
But at four forty-nine in the morning,

120
00:04:17,162 --> 00:04:18,787
they were switched off.

121
00:04:18,847 --> 00:04:20,501
For more than three hours,

122
00:04:20,501 --> 00:04:23,904
there was no automatic brake at all.

123
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:25,781
Without the brake,

124
00:04:25,781 --> 00:04:27,380
the price ran.

125
00:04:27,410 --> 00:04:29,806
From sixty thousand to seventy thousand

126
00:04:29,806 --> 00:04:31,930
dollars in under an hour.

127
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:32,330
Then,

128
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:34,154
in just twenty-four minutes,

129
00:04:34,154 --> 00:04:36,410
from seventy thousand to one hundred and

130
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:38,460
one thousand three hundred and sixty-five

131
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:39,470
dollars.

132
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:41,638
The price had doubled since the close

133
00:04:41,638 --> 00:04:43,500
of the day before.

134
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:44,695
In London,

135
00:04:44,695 --> 00:04:46,990
a board member of the exchange woke

136
00:04:46,990 --> 00:04:48,750
up around half past five,

137
00:04:48,750 --> 00:04:50,255
checked the prices,

138
00:04:50,255 --> 00:04:52,130
and saw the numbers.

139
00:04:52,383 --> 00:04:54,632
Let me draw the morning of March

140
00:04:54,632 --> 00:04:55,513
eighth.

141
00:04:55,753 --> 00:04:57,933
Here is the price on Friday.

142
00:04:58,063 --> 00:04:59,559
Here is Monday's close,

143
00:04:59,559 --> 00:05:01,933
sixty-six percent higher.

144
00:05:02,173 --> 00:05:04,025
And here is the early hours of

145
00:05:04,025 --> 00:05:04,696
Tuesday,

146
00:05:04,696 --> 00:05:07,010
climbing to more than one hundred thousand

147
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:08,073
dollars.

148
00:05:08,483 --> 00:05:10,773
Now look at the bars underneath.

149
00:05:10,813 --> 00:05:12,403
The margin calls.

150
00:05:12,403 --> 00:05:14,613
Three and a half billion on Friday.

151
00:05:14,613 --> 00:05:16,463
Five billion on Monday.

152
00:05:16,463 --> 00:05:18,119
And on Tuesday morning,

153
00:05:18,119 --> 00:05:20,159
almost twenty billion.

154
00:05:20,417 --> 00:05:23,005
At seven thirty in the morning,

155
00:05:23,005 --> 00:05:25,697
the exchange's managers met.

156
00:05:25,947 --> 00:05:27,929
They had less than an hour to

157
00:05:27,929 --> 00:05:29,095
decide.

158
00:05:34,750 --> 00:05:37,113
Can a market just decide that trades

159
00:05:37,113 --> 00:05:38,895
never happened?

160
00:05:43,750 --> 00:05:46,800
The numbers on the table were frightening.

161
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:48,792
That morning's margin calls on nickel

162
00:05:48,792 --> 00:05:51,227
totalled nineteen point seven billion

163
00:05:51,227 --> 00:05:52,230
dollars.

164
00:05:52,230 --> 00:05:54,181
According to the regulator,

165
00:05:54,181 --> 00:05:56,151
calls on that scale were likely to

166
00:05:56,151 --> 00:05:58,516
push several members of the exchange into

167
00:05:58,516 --> 00:05:59,510
default.

168
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:01,705
And when a member defaults,

169
00:06:01,705 --> 00:06:03,967
the losses can spread through the clearing

170
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:06,075
house to everyone else.

171
00:06:06,317 --> 00:06:07,557
At eight fifteen,

172
00:06:07,557 --> 00:06:10,687
the exchange suspended nickel trading.

173
00:06:10,767 --> 00:06:11,769
Later that day,

174
00:06:11,769 --> 00:06:13,187
it went further.

175
00:06:13,227 --> 00:06:16,123
It cancelled every nickel trade made since

176
00:06:16,123 --> 00:06:16,823
midnight,

177
00:06:16,823 --> 00:06:18,994
worth about thirteen point four billion

178
00:06:18,994 --> 00:06:20,077
dollars.

179
00:06:20,357 --> 00:06:22,262
The market stayed shut for more than

180
00:06:22,262 --> 00:06:22,952
a week,

181
00:06:22,952 --> 00:06:25,267
and reopened on March sixteenth.

182
00:06:25,267 --> 00:06:27,373
Night trading from Asia did not return

183
00:06:27,373 --> 00:06:29,026
for a whole year.

184
00:06:29,283 --> 00:06:31,361
The exchange chose midnight as the

185
00:06:31,361 --> 00:06:32,115
cut-off,

186
00:06:32,115 --> 00:06:35,053
not the moment the brakes came off.

187
00:06:35,293 --> 00:06:37,473
Everything traded from the start of the

188
00:06:37,473 --> 00:06:38,643
day was wiped out,

189
00:06:38,643 --> 00:06:41,423
as if the morning had never happened.

190
00:06:41,663 --> 00:06:43,755
And the billions in margin that the

191
00:06:43,755 --> 00:06:45,847
morning's prices had created simply

192
00:06:45,847 --> 00:06:47,842
disappeared with it.

193
00:06:48,083 --> 00:06:50,493
Think about what that meant.

194
00:06:50,493 --> 00:06:52,690
A trader who had sold nickel at

195
00:06:52,690 --> 00:06:55,130
one hundred thousand dollars that morning

196
00:06:55,130 --> 00:06:57,683
suddenly had no trade at all.

197
00:06:57,863 --> 00:06:59,717
And a company caught short,

198
00:06:59,717 --> 00:07:01,572
facing billions in losses,

199
00:07:01,572 --> 00:07:04,154
was suddenly spared the worst prices of

200
00:07:04,154 --> 00:07:05,223
the day.

201
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:08,065
The cancellation saved the market from a

202
00:07:08,065 --> 00:07:09,543
chain of defaults.

203
00:07:09,653 --> 00:07:11,826
But it also took real profits away

204
00:07:11,826 --> 00:07:14,382
from people who had done nothing wrong.

205
00:07:14,617 --> 00:07:17,839
Some of those people went to court.

206
00:07:23,483 --> 00:07:26,952
Was it legal to erase the trades?

207
00:07:32,483 --> 00:07:34,806
The American hedge fund Elliott said the

208
00:07:34,806 --> 00:07:36,918
cancellation had cost it about four

209
00:07:36,918 --> 00:07:39,241
hundred and fifty-six million dollars in

210
00:07:39,241 --> 00:07:40,263
profits.

211
00:07:40,563 --> 00:07:42,514
The trading firm Jane Street put its

212
00:07:42,514 --> 00:07:45,553
loss at about fifteen million dollars.

213
00:07:45,973 --> 00:07:47,990
They argued that the market had not

214
00:07:47,990 --> 00:07:49,761
been disorderly at all,

215
00:07:49,761 --> 00:07:51,779
that the price was simply the result

216
00:07:51,779 --> 00:07:53,998
of a squeeze on traders like Tsingshan,

217
00:07:53,998 --> 00:07:55,791
and that the exchange had acted

218
00:07:55,791 --> 00:07:57,288
unlawfully.

219
00:07:57,550 --> 00:08:00,186
In November twenty twenty-three,

220
00:08:00,186 --> 00:08:02,822
the High Court in London disagreed,

221
00:08:02,822 --> 00:08:05,020
and dismissed the case.

222
00:08:05,210 --> 00:08:07,163
The traders had signed up to the

223
00:08:07,163 --> 00:08:08,443
exchange's rules,

224
00:08:08,443 --> 00:08:10,395
and those rules gave it the power

225
00:08:10,395 --> 00:08:12,080
to cancel trades.

226
00:08:12,310 --> 00:08:14,508
In October twenty twenty-four,

227
00:08:14,508 --> 00:08:17,550
the Court of Appeal upheld that decision.

228
00:08:17,690 --> 00:08:19,153
The cancellation,

229
00:08:19,153 --> 00:08:20,120
the courts found,

230
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:21,712
had been lawful.

231
00:08:21,950 --> 00:08:24,192
Let me draw the dilemma the exchange

232
00:08:24,192 --> 00:08:25,240
faced.

233
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:26,484
Option one,

234
00:08:26,484 --> 00:08:28,550
let the trades stand.

235
00:08:28,630 --> 00:08:30,610
Then several members might default,

236
00:08:30,610 --> 00:08:32,210
and the damage could spread through the

237
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:34,010
whole clearing system.

238
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:35,382
Option two,

239
00:08:35,382 --> 00:08:37,420
cancel the trades.

240
00:08:37,430 --> 00:08:38,959
The system survives,

241
00:08:38,959 --> 00:08:40,954
but some traders lose money that they

242
00:08:40,954 --> 00:08:42,440
had fairly won.

243
00:08:42,730 --> 00:08:44,628
The arrow from each option leads to

244
00:08:44,628 --> 00:08:45,860
a loser.

245
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:48,004
The exchange chose the loser it thought

246
00:08:48,004 --> 00:08:49,954
the market could survive.

247
00:08:50,217 --> 00:08:53,347
The courts said the decision was lawful.

248
00:08:53,457 --> 00:08:56,277
The regulator asked a different question.

249
00:08:56,327 --> 00:08:58,494
How did it get that far?

250
00:09:04,150 --> 00:09:07,088
Who was responsible for the chaos?

251
00:09:13,150 --> 00:09:14,943
In March twenty twenty-five,

252
00:09:14,943 --> 00:09:17,439
Britain's Financial Conduct Authority

253
00:09:17,439 --> 00:09:19,935
fined the London Metal Exchange about

254
00:09:19,935 --> 00:09:21,470
twelve million dollars.

255
00:09:21,470 --> 00:09:23,831
It found that the exchange's policies,

256
00:09:23,831 --> 00:09:26,213
controls and training had not prepared it

257
00:09:26,213 --> 00:09:28,546
for extreme volatility.

258
00:09:28,783 --> 00:09:31,664
The regulator also described the culture

259
00:09:31,664 --> 00:09:33,593
behind the decisions.

260
00:09:33,793 --> 00:09:36,279
The prevailing philosophy at the exchange,

261
00:09:36,279 --> 00:09:36,838
it said,

262
00:09:36,838 --> 00:09:38,765
was that trading should not be restricted

263
00:09:38,765 --> 00:09:41,443
even in times of extreme volatility,

264
00:09:41,443 --> 00:09:44,163
unless the market became disorderly.

265
00:09:44,333 --> 00:09:46,593
Its price bands had been designed mainly

266
00:09:46,593 --> 00:09:47,927
to catch mistakes,

267
00:09:47,927 --> 00:09:49,961
a fat finger on a keyboard or

268
00:09:49,961 --> 00:09:52,123
a rogue computer program.

269
00:09:52,123 --> 00:09:53,269
Not a real squeeze,

270
00:09:53,269 --> 00:09:55,117
in the middle of the night,

271
00:09:55,117 --> 00:09:56,887
with billions at stake.

272
00:09:57,150 --> 00:09:59,526
The notice describes an exchange that

273
00:09:59,526 --> 00:10:01,790
could not see the risk building.

274
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,680
Its overnight team in Hong Kong had

275
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:05,351
switched off the price bands without

276
00:10:05,351 --> 00:10:07,418
consulting senior management,

277
00:10:07,418 --> 00:10:09,716
and had never been trained to escalate

278
00:10:09,716 --> 00:10:11,360
such a decision.

279
00:10:11,770 --> 00:10:13,504
And it could not see the private

280
00:10:13,504 --> 00:10:14,566
positions that,

281
00:10:14,566 --> 00:10:15,628
it later found,

282
00:10:15,628 --> 00:10:17,578
were a material cause of the price

283
00:10:17,578 --> 00:10:18,395
rise.

284
00:10:18,650 --> 00:10:21,239
So what does the nickel crisis teach

285
00:10:21,239 --> 00:10:22,990
about markets?

286
00:10:23,010 --> 00:10:25,138
That a price is only as real

287
00:10:25,138 --> 00:10:27,600
as the system behind it.

288
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:30,175
When enough people bet one way,

289
00:10:30,175 --> 00:10:32,529
and the bets are hidden from view,

290
00:10:32,529 --> 00:10:34,645
a market can break in a few

291
00:10:34,645 --> 00:10:35,510
hours.

292
00:10:35,650 --> 00:10:38,513
The exchange saved itself by rewriting the

293
00:10:38,513 --> 00:10:39,590
morning.

